    "A chemist needed 4-hydroxy-2-butanone, a building block for several drugs, from 1,3-butanediol. Oxidizing the diol directly with PCC gave a mixture, because PCC oxidizes both primary and secondary alcohols. Instead, she used the route in Figure 1. <i>tert</i>-Butyldimethylsilyl chloride (TBSCl) reacts with an alcohol in the presence of imidazole, a mild base, to form a TBS ether (R–O–Si(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>). Because the silicon atom carries a bulky <i>tert</i>-butyl group, TBSCl reacts much faster with unhindered primary alcohols than with secondary ones.",
    scheme("Figure 1 A protect–oxidize–deprotect route", [
        "OCCC(C)O", ("→", "TBSCl", "imidazole"), "CC(O)CCO[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C", "\n", ("→", "PCC", "CH₂Cl₂"),
        "CC(=O)CCO[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C", ("→", "TBAF", "THF"), "CC(=O)CCO"],
        labels=["1,3-butanediol", "TBS ether", "protected ketone", "4-hydroxy-2-butanone"]),
